Here's the deep dive. All numbers are from simulations I just ran.

## The reversal that defines the chapter

Chapter 21 asks: **given these rules, what will people do?** Chapter 24 asks the inverse: **given what I want to happen, what rules should I write?** Hence the nickname *reverse game theory*. It earned Hurwicz, Maskin and Myerson the 2007 Nobel, Roth and Shapley the 2012, Milgrom and Wilson the 2020 — three prizes in thirteen years, which tells you how much of modern applied economics this is.

The designer faces one of two handicaps:

- **Hidden information:** you don't know what people value. They'll tell you whatever serves them.
- **Hidden action:** you can't see what people do. They'll do whatever you pay for.

And one beautiful result organizes the whole field. The **revelation principle**: anything you can achieve with *any* mechanism, however baroque, you can achieve with a simple one where everybody just tells the truth. So the only question worth asking is: **can I make honesty the best policy?** If no truthful mechanism does what you want, nothing does.

