# Part 1. Hidden action: you get exactly what you pay for

Start here because it's the most intuitive and the most immediately useful.

**The convict ships.** Britain shipped convicts to Australia from 1787, paying captains **per convict loaded** in England. Mortality was appalling. Then the payment was changed to **per convict landed alive**, and it collapsed. The figures get repeated with varying precision — commonly as a fall from double-digit percentages to around one percent — so treat the exact numbers with care, but the structure is unambiguous and the mechanism cost nothing. Nobody inspected a ship. Nobody appealed to captains' consciences. The payment clause did all the work.

The general principle has an unpleasant edge: **you cannot reward what you cannot measure, and you will get precisely what you measure, including the parts you didn't mean.**

| Mechanism | What it bought | What it also bought |
|---|---|---|
| Insurance deductibles | Fewer frivolous claims | Underuse of genuinely needed care |
| Sales commissions | More selling | Wells Fargo's millions of fake accounts |
| Test-score accountability | Attention to tested skills | Teaching to the test; cheating scandals |
| Soviet nail quotas **by weight** | Heavy nails | Giant useless nails |
| Soviet nail quotas **by count** | Many nails | Tiny useless nails |
| Equity vesting over four years | Retention | Four-year thinking |
| Paying researchers per publication | Publications | Salami-sliced papers |

Holmström and Milgrom's **multitasking** result is the formal version: when a job has several dimensions and you can only measure some, strong incentives on the measurable ones actively damage the rest. The practical implication is counterintuitive — **when the job is complex and only partly observable, weak incentives are better than strong ones.** That's a serious argument for flat salaries in jobs where quality is hard to see, and it's why the sharpest incentive schemes show up where output is simple and countable (fruit picking, call volume) and perform badly in research, teaching and management.

