# Part 2. Auctions

## The four formats and the two surprises

| Format | How it works | Strategically equivalent to |
|---|---|---|
| **English** (ascending) | Price rises, last bidder standing wins | Second-price sealed bid |
| **Dutch** (descending) | Price falls, first to accept wins | First-price sealed bid |
| **First-price sealed** | Highest bid wins, pays their bid | Dutch |
| **Second-price sealed (Vickrey)** | Highest bid wins, pays the **second** highest bid | English |

**Vickrey's insight (1961):** in a second-price auction, **bidding your true value is a dominant strategy** — optimal no matter what anyone else does. The intuition is clean. Raising your bid above your value only changes anything in cases where you win at a price above your value, which you didn't want. Lowering it only changes anything in cases where you lose an auction you'd have been happy to win. You never pay your own bid, so your bid's only job is deciding *whether* you win, and you want to win exactly when the price is below your value.

Verified — your value is 70, three rivals with values uniform on 0–100:

| Your bid | Expected profit, second-price | Expected profit, first-price |
|---|---|---|
| 50 | 4.03 | 2.48 |
| 55 | 4.75 | **2.48 ← best** |
| 65 | 5.80 | 1.37 |
| **70** | **5.97 ← best** | 0.00 |
| 75 | 5.77 | −2.11 |
| 90 | 1.79 | −14.58 |

Two practical readings beyond "truth is optimal":

- **The second-price curve is flat near the top.** Bidding 65 or 75 instead of 70 costs you almost nothing. The mechanism is *forgiving*. This is the real-world argument for proxy bidding on eBay and for sealed second-price in any setting with amateur participants: you don't need sophistication to do nearly as well as an expert.
- **First-price punishes error brutally and requires you to know how many rivals you face.** Shade too little and you win worthless victories; shade too much and you lose good ones. It transfers money from the unsophisticated to the sophisticated, which is a *distributional* property of the rules, not of the participants.

## Revenue equivalence, and what breaks it

With values drawn independently, all four formats raise the same expected revenue:

| Bidders | Second-price revenue | First-price revenue | Theory: (n−1)/(n+1)×100 |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2 | 33.36 | 33.37 | 33.33 |
| 3 | 49.99 | 50.03 | 50.00 |
| 5 | 66.65 | 66.64 | 66.67 |
| 10 | 81.80 | 81.81 | 81.82 |

The formats differ completely in how they *look* and not at all in what they *raise*. The second-price auction charges less per winner but induces honest, higher bids; the first-price auction charges the winner's full bid but induces shading. These exactly cancel.

This is the single most useful auction result and also the one to be most suspicious of, because it fails whenever its assumptions do:

- **Risk-averse bidders** → first-price raises *more* (bidders shade less to avoid losing).
- **Common values** (everyone's bidding on the same underlying thing) → the winner's curse appears; see below.
- **Collusion** → the English auction is the most vulnerable, because bidders can watch each other and signal.
- **Budget constraints, asymmetric bidders, resale** → all break it.
- **Entry** → which brings us to the best practical finding in the chapter.

## One more bidder beats the cleverest reserve price

A reserve price (minimum acceptable bid) raises revenue because it forces bidders to pay more when values happen to be low — at the cost of sometimes not selling at all. I found the optimal reserve by brute force, then compared it to simply attracting one additional bidder:

| Bidders | No reserve | Best reserve | Revenue with best reserve | Revenue with **one more bidder**, no reserve |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2 | 33.33 | 50 | 41.68 | **50.00** |
| 3 | 49.99 | 50 | 53.16 | **60.07** |
| 5 | 66.72 | 50 | 67.24 | **71.43** |

This is the **Bulow–Klemperer theorem**, and it's the most actionable thing here: *a plain English auction with n+1 bidders beats the theoretically optimal mechanism with n bidders.* Note also that the optimal reserve is 50 regardless of the number of bidders, and that its value shrinks fast as the field grows.

Translation for anyone selling anything: **stop optimizing the squeeze and go find another buyer.** Effort spent on marketing the sale, lowering entry barriers, and making the process easy to join dominates effort spent on clever pricing rules. Klemperer's broader point from advising real auction designs is the same — the failures were almost always about insufficient entry and collusion, never about choosing the wrong format from the textbook.

## The winner's curse

Now change one assumption: everyone is bidding on the *same* value (an oil tract holds a definite amount of oil; a company has a definite worth), and each bidder has an unbiased but noisy estimate. True value 100, estimates with standard deviation 20:

| Bidders | Average winning estimate | Winner's profit, second-price | Winner's profit, first-price |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2 | 111.3 | **+11.30** | −11.24 |
| 5 | 123.2 | −9.89 | −23.25 |
| 10 | 130.7 | −20.00 | −30.80 |
| 20 | 137.3 | **−28.16** | −37.35 |

Everyone's estimate is unbiased. Nobody is irrational. And bidding your honest estimate loses money, worse as the field grows, because **winning is itself bad news**: the winner is, by construction, whoever was most over-optimistic. "Congratulations, you've paid more than anybody else in the world thought it was worth."

The comparative static is the part that breaks intuition: **in a common-value auction, more competitors should make you bid *less*.** Exactly backwards from the private-value case, where more rivals mean bid closer to your value.

This was discovered in practice, not theory — Capen, Clapp and Campbell's 1971 paper came out of ARCO noticing that oil companies were systematically overpaying for offshore leases. It is the standard account of acquisition premiums that destroy shareholder value, of free-agent contracts that look insane in year three, and of book advances that never earn out.

## Three stories from the real world

**New Zealand, 1990.** The government sold broadcasting licences by sealed-bid second-price, properly following the theory. Then the results were published. In widely reported cases the winning bid was NZ$100,000 and the price paid NZ$6; another bid in the millions paid a few thousand. The mechanism performed exactly as designed, and it was a political catastrophe. **Lesson: a mechanism has to survive publication.** The gap between the bid and the price is invisible in an English auction and glaring in a sealed second-price one, which is a reason real-world designers favour ascending formats even where theory says they're equivalent.

**FCC spectrum auctions, 1990s.** The simultaneous multiple-round ascending design (Milgrom, Wilson, McAfee) worked well, but bidders found a channel the designers hadn't closed: they encoded messages in the **trailing digits of their bids** — using the last three digits to point at a market number — to signal "stay out of my city and I'll stay out of yours." A mechanism with full bid transparency had accidentally built a chat room. The fix was click-box bidding, which eliminated the usable digits. **Lesson: every piece of information your mechanism reveals is a coordination device for the people you're bidding against each other.**

**Google's ad auction.** Google runs a generalized second-price auction, which — unlike true Vickrey — is *not* truthful. They kept it anyway: it's simpler to explain, bidders understand it, and it's robust in ways VCG isn't. Facebook did adopt VCG. That two of the most sophisticated auction operators on earth made opposite choices tells you the theory underdetermines the practice.

