# Part 3. Voting rules: the rule picks the winner

This is the demonstration that should change how you read election results. I searched for a ballot profile where four standard rules give four different winners. Here it is — 122 voters, nobody lying, nobody changing their mind:

| Voters | Ranking |
|---|---|
| 38 | C > A > D > B |
| 28 | B > D > A > C |
| 27 | D > B > A > C |
| 26 | A > D > B > C |
| 3 | C > D > A > B |

| Rule | Winner | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Plurality** | **C** | Most first-place votes (41) |
| **Runoff / IRV** | **B** | Top two are C and B; B wins 81–41 |
| **Borda count** | **D** | Highest average rank (233 points) |
| **Condorcet** | **A** | Beats B 67–55, C 81–41, D 64–58 head-to-head |

Four rules, four winners, one set of ballots. And each has a legitimate claim: C has the largest base, B wins a head-to-head against the leader, D is everyone's acceptable choice, A beats every single rival one-on-one.

**The outcome was determined by the rule, not by the voters.** Which means that whoever sets the rules has already done much of the choosing, and that "the people's will" is not a well-defined object once there are more than two options.

## And majority rule can produce no winner at all

Three voters, three candidates:

> A > B > C | B > C > A | C > A > B

A beats B 2–1, B beats C 2–1, **and C beats A 2–1.** The **Condorcet paradox**: collective preferences cycle even though every individual's are perfectly consistent.

The consequence is **agenda power**. In a cycle, whoever controls the order of pairwise votes controls the winner — vote A against B first and C wins the final; vote B against C first and A wins. This is not academic; it's the mechanics of legislative procedure, and the reason committee chairs and the ordering of amendments matter so much. The "killer amendment" is this theorem deployed deliberately.

## Each rule's characteristic pathology

| Rule | Fails at | Real-world instance |
|---|---|---|
| **Plurality** | Spoilers, vote-splitting, winners opposed by a majority | Any three-way race with two similar candidates |
| **Runoff / IRV** | **Non-monotonicity** — ranking a candidate *higher* can make them lose | The 2009 Burlington, Vermont mayoral race is the standard documented case |
| **Borda** | Manipulation by **burying** — rank the real threat last | Borda himself reportedly said his scheme was "only for honest men" |
| **Condorcet methods** | May have no winner (the cycle above) | — |
| **Approval / score** | Strategic thresholds; how approving to be | — |

And the two impossibility theorems that explain why this list has no bottom:

- **Arrow (1951):** with three or more options, no ranked voting rule can simultaneously satisfy unanimity, independence of irrelevant alternatives, and non-dictatorship.
- **Gibbard–Satterthwaite:** every non-dictatorial, deterministic rule over three or more outcomes is **manipulable**. Strategic voting isn't a defect of a particular system; it's unavoidable.

The constructive flip side is **May's theorem**: with exactly *two* options, simple majority rule is uniquely characterized by a set of entirely reasonable properties. **All the trouble starts at three.** Which is a genuinely useful piece of institutional advice: if you can decompose a decision into binary choices, voting theory is on your side; if you can't, pick your rule deliberately and in advance, before anyone knows which option it will favour.

