# Part 4. Pivot mechanisms: pay the externality you cause

Back to hidden information, but now for a *public* decision where there's no price to pay. A bridge costs 90, three neighbours share the cost equally (30 each), and only they know what it's worth to them. Ask them and they'll exaggerate — up if they want it, down if they don't.

**The Clarke / Vivrey–Clarke–Groves pivot mechanism:** build it if reported net values sum to positive, and charge each person **only if they changed the decision** — in which case they pay the amount by which they overrode everyone else.

| Reported values | Build? | Pivot taxes | Extra money collected |
|---|---|---|---|
| 50, 40, 10 | yes | 10, 0, 0 | 10 |
| 20, 20, 20 | no | 0, 0, 0 | 0 |
| 80, 10, 10 | yes | 40, 0, 0 | 40 |
| 40, 35, 30 | yes | 0, 0, 0 | 0 |

**Why honesty is optimal:** exaggerating only matters when it flips the decision, and if it flips the decision, you pay the full amount of harm you imposed on everyone else — which is more than you gained, exactly when you lied. Understating only risks losing something you wanted at a price you'd have been happy to pay. In the third row the enthusiast with value 80 gets the bridge but pays 40 extra, because the other two together didn't want it.

**And here's the catch.** Across 5,000 random neighbourhoods, somebody was pivotal 49% of the time, and the mechanism collected an average of **5.38 in pivot taxes beyond the cost shares**. That money cannot be refunded. The instant you rebate it, the rebate becomes part of everyone's calculation and honesty breaks. **It must be burned.** This is the **Green–Laffont impossibility**: no mechanism is simultaneously efficient, truthful, and budget-balanced.

## The same wall, in the simplest possible market

One buyer, one seller, each knowing only their own number, both uniform on 0–100. Trade should happen whenever the buyer values the item more.

- Efficient trade is possible **49.9%** of the time, with an average gain of **33.30**
- Pivot prices that keep *both* honest: the buyer pays the seller's cost, the seller receives the buyer's value
- So every trade needs an **outside subsidy** averaging **16.62** per attempted trade

This is **Myerson–Satterthwaite** (1983): for bilateral trade with two-sided private information, no mechanism is efficient, truthful, voluntary and budget-balanced. One of them has to go.

This is a deep and slightly deflating result: **some gains from trade are unreachable, no matter how clever the institution.** Real markets give up efficiency — haggling, posted prices and take-it-or-leave-it offers all leave some mutually beneficial trades undone, and that isn't a failure anyone can design away. It also explains why so much real deal-making involves brokers, standardized contracts and reputations: all of those are ways of reducing the private information that causes the problem in the first place.

## Why VCG is rarer than its elegance suggests

It's truthful, efficient, and beautiful, and it's mostly not used. Reasons, each of which is a design lesson:

- **Budget problems** — surpluses that must be wasted, or deficits needing subsidy
- **Fragile to collusion and shill bidding** — a small ring can extract a lot
- **Breaks under budget constraints** — truthful bidding presumes you can pay what you bid
- **Computationally brutal** in combinatorial settings — you must solve the allocation problem once per participant
- **Looks indefensible when published** — the New Zealand problem
- **Hard to explain**, and a mechanism nobody understands gets no honest reports anyway

