# Part 5. Where mechanism design has actually changed the world

The field's best work isn't in auctions; it's in matching, where money is unavailable or forbidden.

**Medical residencies.** The National Resident Matching Program uses deferred acceptance (Gale–Shapley): applicants propose, programs tentatively hold the best offers so far, rejected applicants propose again. The result is **stable** — no doctor and hospital both prefer each other to their assignment — and it's **strategy-proof for the proposing side**, so applicants should simply rank honestly. Roth's analysis of why the pre-1950s unravelling market failed, and his later redesign to handle married couples matching together, is the model case of the theory earning its keep.

**School choice, and the mechanism that punished honesty.** The old Boston system assigned as many students as possible to their first choice, then second choices, and so on. The trap: if you listed a popular school first and missed, you'd arrive at your second choice *after* everyone who ranked it first — so naming your true favourite could cost you your second favourite too. Parents had to strategize, and the parents who strategized well were the ones with time, information and English fluency. **The mechanism was a tax on honesty, levied on the least sophisticated families.** Boston replaced it with deferred acceptance in 2005; New York's high school match followed similar logic. Honest ranking is now safe, which is the entire point.

**Kidney exchange.** Buying organs is illegal nearly everywhere, so price can't clear this market. Instead: find cycles (my donor is compatible with you, yours with me) and chains starting from an altruistic donor. This is pure mechanism design — the algorithm is the market — and it has produced thousands of transplants that otherwise would not have happened.

Roth's term for the constraint that makes kidney exchange necessary is **repugnance**: some trades are socially forbidden regardless of efficiency. A designer who ignores that constraint produces an elegant mechanism nobody will adopt. It belongs on the list with budget balance and participation as a real design constraint, not a confusion to be corrected.

